= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $C$ denote the addition-commutativity <first-order sentence> and put $d=\ulcorner\neg C\urcorner$. In the ordinary checker for PA derivations, replace the axiom test $\operatorname{Ax}_{PA}(u)$ by
$$
\operatorname{Ax}_S(u)\equiv\operatorname{Ax}_{PA}(u)\vee u=\bar d.
$$
Keep all tests for sequence formation, earlier lines, logical axiom schemes and <inference rules> unchanged. This gives a <formal proof> checker $Q_S(u,w)$ which recognizes exactly ordinary $S$-derivations. The finite syntactic modification is arithmetizable by the same bounded coding apparatus as the original checker.

For the proof-order comparison in the next part, make the choice of coding explicit. Reserve code $0$ for the one-line derivation of the newly added axiom $\neg C$, and give an ordinary derivation with code $w$ the new code $w+1$. A corresponding bounded relation is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Prov}_S(u,v)\equiv
(v=0\wedge u=\bar d)\vee(\exists w\le v)(v=w+1\wedge Q_S(u,w)).}
$$
Every code has a specified decoding, every genuine <formal proof> is represented, and no false <formal proof> is admitted. The reserved code duplicates one genuine derivation, which is harmless. This is one explicit arithmetized <arithmetical proof relation> obtainable from the PA checker by the requested axiom change and a simple renumbering. Stating the proof-order convention matters because a Rosser <predicate symbol> uses code order, not merely the existence of <formal proofs>.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <arithmetical proof relation> and coding just constructed. A <formal proof> of $C$ cannot have code $0$, since that code was reserved for $\neg C$. Every candidate code $v$ for a <formal proof> of $C$ is therefore positive. But code $0\le v$ is already a <formal proof> of $\neg C$, so the no-earlier-negation condition fails for every candidate. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb N\models\neg\operatorname{Pr}_S^*(\ulcorner C\urcorner).}
$$
PA proves $C$ by <induction>, while $S$ has $\neg C$ as an added axiom. Thus $S$ is inconsistent and its ordinary <arithmetical provability predicate> is true of every <first-order sentence>, including $C$. The displayed negative Rosser assertion can nevertheless be true, because the Rosser condition compares the order of opposite <formal proofs> rather than asking for their mere existence.

There is an important coding qualification. The PDF refers to a course <formal proof> coding but does not specify its numerical ordering. The truth of this Rosser assertion for an inconsistent <first-order theory> is not coding-invariant, so <formal proof> length alone cannot establish it for an unspecified fixed coding. For example, a different legitimate numbering could reserve code $0$ for a known PA <formal proof> of $C$, code $1$ for the new axiom $\neg C$, and higher codes for all other derivations. Then code $0$ would witness $\operatorname{Pr}_S^*(\ulcorner C\urcorner)$ instead. The explicit allowed construction in part (i) supplies a convention under which the requested assertion holds; interpreting it as a claim about some externally fixed course numbering requires that numbering's corresponding <order property>.

