= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <Hilbert-Bernays-Löb derivability conditions> for an <arithmetical provability predicate>. Abbreviate $P_S(\ulcorner A\urcorner)$ by $P A$. The conditions needed below are
$$
S\vdash A\Longrightarrow S\vdash P A,\qquad
S\vdash P(A\to B)\to(P A\to P B),\qquad
S\vdash P A\to P P A.
$$
These are the usual theoremhood, distribution and positive-introspection conditions intended by a standard <arithmetical provability predicate>; arbitrary <predicate symbols> merely agreeing with provability externally need not satisfy them.

Since $S\vdash\neg Y$, it proves $Y\to G$. The first two conditions give $S\vdash P Y\to P G$. For the converse, the fixed-point equivalence gives $S\vdash G\to(P G\to Y)$. Applying theoremhood and distribution gives
$$
S\vdash P G\to P(P G\to Y).
$$
<Positive introspection> also gives $P G\to P P G$, and distribution combines these to yield $S\vdash P G\to P Y$. Thus $S\vdash P G\leftrightarrow P Y$. Combining with $G\leftrightarrow\neg P G$ proves
$$
\boxed{S\vdash G\leftrightarrow\neg P Y.}
$$
If $S$ proved $\neg P Y$, it would prove $G$. The theoremhood condition would then give $P G$, while the fixed-point equivalence gives $\neg P G$. Therefore <first-order consistency> implies
$$
\boxed{S\nvdash\neg P Y.}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $T=S\cup\{\neg X\}$ and assume $S\vdash P_S(\ulcorner X\urcorner)\to X$. In $T$, contraposition against its extra axiom gives $\neg P_S(\ulcorner X\urcorner)$.

Classical logic proves $(\neg X\to X)\to X$. By the <derivability conditions>,
$$
S\vdash P_S(\ulcorner\neg X\to X\urcorner)\to P_S(\ulcorner X\urcorner).
$$
Compose this with the supplied formalized deduction implication from $P_T(\ulcorner X\urcorner)$ to its left-hand side. Then $T$ proves
$$
P_T(\ulcorner X\urcorner)\to P_S(\ulcorner X\urcorner),
$$
and therefore
$$
T\vdash\neg P_T(\ulcorner X\urcorner).
$$
The other supplied assumption says this is impossible if $T$ is <first-order consistent>. Thus $T$ is inconsistent. By the deduction theorem, inconsistency after adjoining $\neg X$ yields $S\vdash\neg\neg X$, hence
$$
\boxed{S\vdash X.}
$$
This establishes Löb's <inference rule> using precisely the strengthened unprovability assumption and the formalized deduction property supplied in the question.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take a <first-order sentence> $Y$ refutable in $S$ and suppose, toward <contradiction>, that a <first-order consistent> $S$ proved $\neg P_S(\ulcorner Y\urcorner)$. <Propositional logic> would then give
$$
S\vdash P_S(\ulcorner Y\urcorner)\to Y.
$$
Löb's inference, applied with $X=Y$, would give $S\vdash Y$, contradicting the existing <formal proof> of $\neg Y$. Hence <first-order consistency> excludes the proposed <formal proof> of non-provability.

Thus the familiar unprovability of the <first-order consistency> statement associated with a refutable <first-order sentence> is a special consequence of the general rule
$$
\boxed{S\vdash(P_S(\ulcorner X\urcorner)\to X)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad S\vdash X.}
$$
The general rule applies to every <first-order sentence>, not just a chosen false or refutable one.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $j=i+n$ with $n\ge1$. <Positive introspection> supplies the theorem $F_k\to F_{k+1}$ for every $k\ge1$, since $F_k$ itself has the form $P_S(\ulcorner F_{k-1}\urcorner)$.

<Induction> on $n$ consequently gives
$$
S\vdash F_{i+1}\to F_{i+n}.
$$
At $n=1$ this is the identity implication. To pass from $n$ to $n+1$, compose the <induction> hypothesis with the positive-introspection implication at $k=i+n$, which is at least one. Composing the result with the assumed $F_j\to F_i$ gives
$$
S\vdash F_{i+1}\to F_i,
$$
i.e. $S\vdash P_S(\ulcorner F_i\urcorner)\to F_i$. Löb's rule now yields
$$
\boxed{S\vdash F_i.}
$$
The index restriction on <positive introspection> has been respected even when $i=0$: the chain begins at $F_{i+1}$, which already is a provability assertion.

