= Solution
{parent=a}

The source's system called $K$ already contains <positive introspection>, so its displayed axioms are those usually called $K4$. Put
$$
B=\square(\square X\to X),\qquad A=(B\to\square X).
$$
We first show that its axioms prove $\square A\to A$, the implication suggested in the hint. From $B$, <positive introspection> gives $\square B$. Distribution applied to $\square A=\square(B\to\square X)$ and $\square B$ gives $\square\square X$. Distribution applied to $B=\square(\square X\to X)$ and this last result gives $\square X$. Thus the theorem-level implications combine by propositional reasoning into
$$
K4\vdash\square A\to(B\to\square X),
$$
which is $K4\vdash\square A\to A$.

Since $S$ contains these axioms, it proves that implication. Its assumed Löb <inference rule>, applied to the <first-order sentence> $A$, yields $S\vdash A$. Unpacking $A$ proves
$$
\boxed{S\vdash\square(\square X\to X)\to\square X.}
$$
This uses necessitation only as part of the ordinary modal theorem rules; no inference of a boxed <temporary assumption> has been made.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $C=\square X\wedge X$. The normal distribution axiom and necessitation of $C\to X$ give $\square C\to\square X$. Combining this with propositional reasoning yields the theorem
$$
X\to(\square C\to C).
$$
Indeed, $X$ together with $\square C$ supplies both conjuncts of $C$. Necessitate this theorem and apply distribution to obtain
$$
\square X\to\square(\square C\to C).
$$
The new Löb axiom at $C$ now gives $\square X\to\square C$. Finally, necessitation and distribution applied to the tautology $C\to\square X$ give $\square C\to\square\square X$. Composition proves
$$
\boxed{GL\vdash\square X\to\square\square X.}
$$
No positive-introspection instance was used in deriving it. The argument shows why that scheme can be dropped when the Löb scheme is added.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<Set> $Y=\square X\to X$. The Löb axiom at $X$ is $\square Y\to\square X$. Therefore $Y$ and $\square Y$ together imply $X$, proving
$$
GL\vdash Y\to(\square Y\to X).
$$
For the converse, the tautology $X\to Y$ gives $\square X\to\square Y$ by necessitation and distribution. Consequently $\square Y\to X$ together with $\square X$ gives $X$, so
$$
GL\vdash(\square Y\to X)\to(\square X\to X),
$$
whose consequent is $Y$. Combining both directions gives an unboxed equivalence as a theorem of GL. Necessitation then yields exactly
$$
\boxed{GL\vdash\square\bigl(Y\leftrightarrow(\square Y\to X)\bigr).}
$$

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Under the arithmetical interpretation $\square A=P_S(\ulcorner A\urcorner)$, the Löb axiom says that a <formal proof> of the local reflection implication for $A$ already entails a <formal proof> of $A$:
$$
P_S(\ulcorner P_S(\ulcorner A\urcorner)\to A\urcorner)\to P_S(\ulcorner A\urcorner).
$$
It is a formalized constraint on self-verification of a <formal proof> system. Externally, <Löb's theorem> says that if the system itself proves its own local reflection implication for $A$, it proves $A$. Distribution reflects closure of <formal proofs> under implication, necessitation reflects that a theorem has an arithmetically recognizable <formal proof>, and <positive introspection> reflects <formal proof> verification inside <arithmetic>.

Taking $A$ to be a refutable <first-order sentence>, especially a <contradiction>, recovers the obstruction behind the second incompleteness theorem: a <first-order consistent> sufficiently strong system cannot prove the corresponding claim that no <formal proof> of that <first-order sentence> exists. This does not identify provability with truth, nor grant the unrestricted factivity principle $\square A\to A$ as a theorem of the system. The Löb axiom is the <characteristic> additional scheme of Gödel–Löb <provability logic>.

