= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $L_A$ expand $L$ by a constant $c_a$ for each $a\in A$, and let $\mathcal A_A$ be the canonical expansion interpreting $c_a$ as $a$. Its <diagram of a structure> is the <set> of all atomic and negated atomic $L_A$-sentences true in $\mathcal A_A$. Its <elementary diagram> is the <set> of all $L_A$-sentences true there:
$$
\operatorname{Diag}(\mathcal A)=\{\theta:\theta\text{ atomic or negated atomic},\ \mathcal A_A\models\theta\},
$$
$$
\operatorname{CDiag}(\mathcal A)=\operatorname{Th}(\mathcal A_A).
$$
A <substructure> inclusion $\mathcal A\subseteq\mathcal B$ means that $A\subseteq B$, constants have the same interpretations, the operations of $\mathcal B$ restrict to those of $\mathcal A$, and each relation restricts to the corresponding relation on $A$.

An <elementary substructure> additionally preserves the truth of every <logical formula> with parameters from $A$:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A\preccurlyeq\mathcal B\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\mathcal A\models\phi(\vec a)\iff\mathcal B\models\phi(\vec a)
\text{ for every }\phi,\vec a\in A.}
$$
Here the right-hand condition includes the <substructure> requirements by applying it to atomic <logical formulas> and their <negations>.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $\mathcal A\subseteq\mathcal B$, interpret each new constant $c_a$ in $\mathcal B$ as the same element $a$. Atomic <logical formulas> and their <negations> then have exactly their <truth values> in $\mathcal A_A$, so the expansion satisfies the <diagram of a structure>. If the inclusion is elementary, every <logical formula> with those named parameters has the same <truth value>, so the expansion satisfies the <elementary diagram>.

Conversely, from any expansion $\mathcal B^+$ satisfying the <diagram of a structure> define
$$
e:A\to B,\qquad e(a)=c_a^{\mathcal B^+}.
$$
The <diagram of a structure> contains $c_a\ne c_{a'}$ whenever $a\ne a'$, so $e$ is <injective>. It contains every true function-value equation, so $e$ commutes with all operations and constants. Positive and negative relation instances ensure both preservation and reflection of the relations. Thus $e$ is an <embedding of first-order structures>. If the expansion satisfies the <elementary diagram>, for every <logical formula> $\phi$ and <tuple> $\vec a$ it also gives
$$
\mathcal A\models\phi(\vec a)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\mathcal B\models\phi(e(\vec a)),
$$
so $e$ is an elementary <embedding of first-order structures>.

**The precise conclusion for an arbitrary expansion is an <embedding of first-order structures>, rather than a literal inclusion of the original carriers.** The source's inclusion notation is correct when one uses the customary identification of $\mathcal A$ with its embedded copy, or requires the expansion to interpret $c_a$ as the actual element $a$ of a carrier already containing $A$. With that naming convention, $e$ is the inclusion and the requested two equivalences follow exactly.

Without the convention the literal wording fails, even in the <first-order language> of <logical equality>: singleton <first-order structures> with carriers $\{0\}$ and $\{1\}$ have a diagram-satisfying expansion by sending the first <first-order structure>'s named point to $1$, but the first carrier is not a <subset> of the second. The <embedding of first-order structures> formulation proved above states the invariant result and makes the intended inclusion formulation precise.

= iii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Downward Lowenheim-Skolem theorem> says that for an $L$-structure $M$ and a <subset> $S\subseteq M$, there is an <elementary substructure> $N\preccurlyeq M$ containing $S$ with
$$
\boxed{|N|\le\max(|S|,|L|,\aleph_0).}
$$
If an infinite cardinal $\kappa$ satisfies $\max(|L|,\aleph_0)\le\kappa\le|M|$, one can in particular obtain size exactly $\kappa$ by starting with a <subset> of that size.

Choose a default element $a_0\in M$. For every <logical formula> $\exists x\phi(x,\vec y)$ choose a <Skolem function> $F_\phi$ which returns a witness in $M$ when one exists, and returns $a_0$ otherwise. Begin with $S$, the interpretations of the <first-order language>'s constants, and $a_0$. Successively adjoin the values of all the original <functions> and these <Skolem functions> on finite <tuples> already chosen. Let $N$ be the <union> of the resulting <countable> sequence of closure stages.

There are at most $\max(|L|,\aleph_0)$ <logical formulas>, and each <function> has finite arity. With $\kappa=\max(|S|,|L|,\aleph_0)$, each closure stage has size at most $\kappa$, and so does their <countable> <union>. Closure under the original <functions> makes $N$ an $L$-substructure. Whenever $M\models\exists x\phi(x,\vec a)$ for parameters in $N$, its chosen Skolem witness belongs to $N$, since the finitely many parameters lie in some common stage. The Tarski–Vaught test therefore gives $N\preccurlyeq M$.

For the exact-cardinality version, choose an initial <set> of $\kappa$ elements, enlarged to contain any prescribed <subset> of size at most $\kappa$. The construction gives an upper bound $\kappa$ while containment gives the matching lower bound.

= iv
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Add a <predicate symbol> $P_\theta$ for each $L$-formula $\theta(\vec x)$, of arity equal to its displayed free-variable <tuple>. Let $T^+$ consist of $T$ together with all defining axioms
$$
\forall\vec x\ (P_\theta(\vec x)\leftrightarrow\theta(\vec x)).
$$
Every <first-order model> of $T$ has a unique such expansion, interpreting the new <predicate symbols> by their defining <sets>. Conversely the reduct of every <first-order model> of $T^+$ satisfies $T$.

Given an arbitrary $L^+$-formula $\psi(\vec x)$, replace each new atomic <predicate symbol> by its original $L$-definition, renaming <bound variables> to avoid capture. This produces an $L$-formula $\psi^-(\vec x)$ with
$$
T^+\models\forall\vec x\ (\psi(\vec x)\leftrightarrow\psi^-(\vec x)).
$$
The defining axiom for that particular <logical formula> now gives an atomic replacement:
$$
\boxed{T^+\models\forall\vec x\ (\psi(\vec x)\leftrightarrow P_{\psi^-}(\vec x)).}
$$
Thus the expanded <first-order theory> eliminates <quantifiers>. For a <first-order sentence>, the same translation is an $L$-sentence, decided by completeness of $T$; hence every expanded <first-order sentence> is decided and $T^+$ is complete. Empty free-variable <tuples> can be handled by zero-ary <predicate symbols>, or by the usual <logical truth> and falsity constants. This definitional expansion is called <Morleyization>.

= 7
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Prime elements> are first-order definable in the <ring> <first-order language>. For instance let $\operatorname{Unit}(u)$ mean $\exists v\ (uv=1)$ and use
$$
\operatorname{Prime}(x)\equiv x\ne0\wedge\neg\operatorname{Unit}(x)\wedge
\forall u\forall v\ (uv=x\to\operatorname{Unit}(u)\vee\operatorname{Unit}(v)).
$$
In the <integer> <ring> this defines <prime elements> up to the harmless choice of sign. Expand the <first-order language> by names for every <integer> and a new constant $c$. Take the <elementary diagram> of $\mathcal Z$ together with
$$
\operatorname{Prime}(c),\qquad c\ne\bar a\quad(a\in\mathbb Z).
$$
Every finite <subset> is <satisfiable> in $\mathcal Z$: choose a positive prime <integer> outside the finitely many excluded values, using the <Euclid's theorem>. <Compactness theorem> gives a <first-order model> of the entire <set>. The complete-diagram argument embeds $\mathcal Z$ elementarily into its reduct; identify that copy with the original <integer> <ring>.

The resulting <elementary extension> contains $c$, a <prime element> unequal to every named standard <integer>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathcal Z\preccurlyeq\mathcal Z^*,\qquad
\mathcal Z^*\models\operatorname{Prime}(c),\qquad c\notin\mathbb Z.}
$$
The construction rules out all standard <integers>, not merely the finite list appearing in any one approximation.

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply downward Löwenheim-Skolem to the extension from part (i), keeping all standard <integers> and the new prime $c$. The <first-order language> and this prescribed <subset> are <countable>, so there is a <countable> <elementary substructure> $N$ containing them. In particular $N\equiv\mathcal Z$ and contains an element outside the standard copy of $\mathbb Z$.

No <ring isomorphism> $N\to\mathcal Z$ can exist. Such an <isomorphism> fixes $0$ and $1$, hence every nonnegative <integer> numeral. It also fixes each negative <integer>, which is the unique solution of $x+\bar n=0$ for the corresponding positive $n$. Consequently it fixes the whole standard copy. The image of $c$ would be some standard <integer> $a$, already the image of that same named standard element, violating <injectivity>.

Thus $N$ and $\mathcal Z$ are two nonisomorphic countably infinite <first-order models> of $T$, so
$$
\boxed{T\text{ is not }\aleph_0\text{-categorical}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Every <integer> is definable without parameters in the <ring> <first-order language>. For $a\ge0$, use $x=\bar a$; for $a=-m<0$, use $x+\bar m=0$. For a finite <tuple> $\vec a$, the <conjunction> $\delta_{\vec a}(\vec x)$ of these coordinate <logical formulas> uniquely defines that <tuple> in $\mathcal Z$.

If $\phi(\vec x)$ belongs to its <complete type>, the <first-order sentence>
$$
\forall\vec x\ (\delta_{\vec a}(\vec x)\to\phi(\vec x))
$$
is true in $\mathcal Z$, since there is only the specified <tuple> to check. It therefore belongs to $T=\operatorname{Th}(\mathcal Z)$. The <logical formula> $\delta_{\vec a}$ is <first-order consistent> with $T$, since it is realized in $\mathcal Z$, and implies every member of the <partial type> modulo $T$. Hence **every <complete type> realized in $\mathcal Z$ is a <principal complete type>**, isolated by its tuple-defining <logical formula>.

= iv
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Take the parameter-free <complete type>
$$
p(x)=\operatorname{tp}^{\mathcal Z^*}(c/\varnothing)
$$
of the nonstandard prime constructed in part (i). It is <first-order consistent> and complete because it consists of exactly the <logical formulas> true of an actual element in a <first-order model> of $T$. It contains $x\ne\bar a$ for every <integer> $a$, and consequently no element of $\mathcal Z$ realizes it. Thus
$$
\boxed{p\text{ is a complete type over }T\text{ omitted in }\mathcal Z.}
$$
It is necessarily a <nonprincipal complete type>. If a <first-order consistent> <logical formula> isolated it, completeness of $T$ would force the existential closure of that <logical formula> to hold in every <first-order model> of $T$, including $\mathcal Z$; a witness there would realize the <partial type>, a <contradiction>. This connects the explicit omission with the hypothesis of the <omitting types theorem>.

= v
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Enumerate the standard positive primes as $(p_n)_{n<\omega}$. For each <subset> $A\subseteq\omega$ consider the <partial type>
$$
q_A(x)=\{x\ne0\}\cup\{\bar p_n\mid x:n\in A\}\cup
\{\neg(\bar p_n\mid x):n\notin A\},
$$
where <divisibility> is the <ring> <logical formula> $\exists y\ (\bar p_ny=x)$.

Every finite <subset> is realized in $\mathbb Z$: take the product of the finitely many primes required to divide $x$, or $1$ if there are none. This is nonzero and is not divisible by any of the distinct finitely many primes required not to divide it. <Compactness theorem> supplies a <complete type> extending $q_A$.

Distinct <subsets> $A,B$ yield distinct complete extensions, because at an index in their symmetric difference they require opposite <divisibility> statements. There are $2^{\aleph_0}$ such <subsets>, so there are at least that many <complete one-types>. There are at most that many because the <first-order language> has only countably many <logical formulas> and a <partial type> is a <set> of them. Hence
$$
\boxed{|S_1(T)|=2^{\aleph_0}.}
$$
In particular the number of <complete one-types> is uncountable, despite every <partial type> realized in the particular <countable> <first-order model> $\mathcal Z$ being a <principal complete type>.

= 8
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Ryll-Nardzewski theorem> says that for a <complete theory> $T$ in a <countable> <first-order language> with infinite <first-order models>, the following are equivalent: $T$ is $\aleph_0$-categorical; for every positive <integer> $n$, the <set> $S_n(T)$ of complete parameter-free $n$-types is finite; every such finite-arity <complete type> is isolated by a <logical formula>. The countable-language hypothesis and the requirement for every finite arity are important.

The <omitting types theorem> says that, for a <first-order consistent> <complete theory> in a <countable> <first-order language>, a <countable> family of <nonprincipal partial types> in finite <tuples> of <logical variables> can all be omitted in a <countable> <first-order model>. Here nonprincipal means that no <logical formula> <first-order consistent> with the <first-order theory> implies every <logical formula> of the <partial type>. Omission means that no <tuple> in the constructed <first-order model> satisfies all <logical formulas> of that <partial type>. The <complete one-type> version is the special case of one <partial type> in one <logical variable>.

= ii
{parent=8}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <complete theory> of <dense linear orders without endpoints> in the <first-order language> $\{<\}$. It eliminates <quantifiers>: existential conditions on one new point reduce to finitely many <logical equality> constraints and interval bounds, and density and absence of endpoints decide whether a suitable point exists. Its countably infinite <first-order models> are all <isomorphic> to the rational order by the usual <back-and-forth method>, extending finite order-preserving maps alternately along enumerations of the two <first-order models>. Thus it is $\aleph_0$-categorical.

To distinguish <first-order models> of size $\aleph_1$, let
$$
M_1=\sum_{\alpha<\omega_1}(\mathbb Q,<),\qquad M_2=M_1+(\mathbb Q,<),
$$
with ordered sums. Both are dense without endpoints and have <cardinality> $\aleph_1$. The first has <cofinality> $\aleph_1$: countably many points lie in a bounded <set> of block indices and cannot be <cofinal>. The second has <cofinality> $\aleph_0$, supplied by its final rational block. <Cofinality of a linear order> is preserved by <isomorphism>, so they are not <isomorphic>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{DLO is }\aleph_0\text{-categorical but not }\aleph_1\text{-categorical}.}
$$
For Ryll-Nardzewski, its $n$-types are precisely the finitely many equality-and-order patterns on $n$ coordinates. Each is isolated by the <conjunction> specifying that pattern, agreeing with the theorem's prediction.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use $ACF_0$, the <complete theory> of <algebraically closed fields> of <characteristic zero>. Its <quantifier elimination> is a standard characterization of this <complete theory>. <Algebraically closed fields> of a fixed <characteristic> are classified up to <isomorphism> by <transcendence degree> over their <prime field>.

For such a <field> $K$,
$$
|K|=\max(\aleph_0,\operatorname{trdeg}_{\mathbb Q}K)
$$
when the <transcendence degree> is infinite, while finite <transcendence degree> also gives a <countable> <field>. This follows by counting <rational functions> in a <transcendence basis> and then algebraic elements over that <field>. Thus every <first-order model> of <cardinality> $\aleph_1$ has <transcendence degree> $\aleph_1$, and the classification gives a unique such <first-order model>.

On the other hand $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and an <algebraic closure> of $\mathbb Q(t)$ are countably infinite <first-order models> with <transcendence degrees> zero and one, so are not <isomorphic>. Hence
$$
\boxed{ACF_0\text{ is }\aleph_1\text{-categorical but not }\aleph_0\text{-categorical}.}
$$
The transcendental <complete one-type> consists of $f(x)\ne0$ for every nonzero <polynomial> over $\mathbb Q$. <Quantifier elimination> makes it complete. It is nonprincipal: any parameter-free one-variable <logical formula> containing a transcendental element defines a cofinite <set>, hence contains algebraic elements as well and cannot isolate this <partial type>. The <first-order model> $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ omits it, concretely illustrating the <omitting types theorem>. The existence of infinitely many algebraic <complete one-types>, together with this <nonprincipal complete type>, also agrees with the failure of the Ryll-Nardzewski finiteness condition.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take the <first-order theory> of an <infinite set> in the <first-order language> of <logical equality> alone, with an axiom asserting at least $n$ distinct elements for each positive <integer> $n$. It is complete and eliminates <quantifiers>: a new <logical variable> either equals a specified old <logical variable> or is required to avoid only finitely many existing values, which an infinite domain permits.

For every infinite cardinal $\kappa$, any two <first-order models> of size $\kappa$ are <isomorphic> because every <bijection> between their underlying <sets> preserves <logical equality>, the whole <first-order language>. Thus
$$
\boxed{T_{\infty}\text{ is categorical in every infinite cardinality}.}
$$
Its complete $n$-types record only the partition of coordinate indices according to <logical equality>. There are finitely many such partitions, and each is isolated by the corresponding <logical equalities> and inequalities. Ryll-Nardzewski therefore recovers <countable> categoricity; there are no nonprincipal complete finite-arity <partial types> to omit in this example.

= d
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The same <complete theory> $T_\infty$ of an infinite pure <set> provides this property directly. For $n$ <logical variables>, each <complete type> specifies exactly which <logical variables> are equal. <First-order consistency> requires an <equivalence relation> on the finite index <set>, and infinitude ensures every such partition is realizable. Hence
$$
\boxed{|S_n(T_\infty)|=B_n<\infty,}
$$
where $B_n$ is the $n$th <Bell number>, counting partitions of an $n$-element <set>. For example the first values are $B_1=1$, $B_2=2$ and $B_3=5$. The isolating <conjunction> lists <logical equality> within blocks and inequality between blocks. This explicitly verifies the hypothesis which Ryll-Nardzewski identifies with $\aleph_0$-categoricity.

= e
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Use the <first-order theory> $T_{\mathbb Z}$ of a <bijection> $f$ with named inverse $g$ and no finite cycles. Its axioms are
$$
\forall x\ f(g(x))=x,\qquad\forall x\ g(f(x))=x,\qquad
\forall x\ f^n(x)\ne x\quad(n=1,2,3,\ldots).
$$
Every <first-order model> is a disjoint <union> of free two-sided <integer> orbits. Write $f^k$ for positive powers of $f$ and for powers of $g$ when $k$ is negative.

This <first-order theory> is complete and eliminates <quantifiers>. Every <first-order term> reduces to $f^k(x_i)$, and atomic <logical formulas> reduce to statements $x_j=f^k(x_i)$. To eliminate an existential <logical variable> in a <conjunction> of literals, first remove self-equalities using the no-finite-cycle axioms. If an equation fixes that <logical variable> as an <integer> translate of another <logical variable>, substitute that value. If no such equation remains, only finitely many points are excluded by the remaining inequalities, so some value exists in the infinite domain. Apply this to <disjunctive normal form> and then induct on <logical formulas>. The resulting parameter-free <first-order sentences> are decided, proving completeness.

For each fixed finite $n$, a <complete type> records a partition of the <logical variables> into common orbits and finitely many <integer> offsets within each block. There are countably many possibilities. <Countable> <first-order models> with one orbit and with two orbits are not <isomorphic>, so the <first-order theory> is not $\aleph_0$-categorical.

Thus this example has the source's explicitly defined countable-type property. In fact it also satisfies ordinary <omega-stability>: over a <countable> parameter <set>, an element is either a specified <integer> translate of a parameter, giving countably many possibilities, or lies in an entirely new orbit, giving one further <complete one-type>. The same finite-orbit-pattern description handles finite <tuples>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{T_{\mathbb Z}\text{ has countably many }n\text{-types for every finite }n,
\text{ but is not }\aleph_0\text{-categorical}.}
$$
The source prints the label “$0$-stable”; the construction meets its stated definition as well as the usual stronger <omega-stability> condition. The difference between countably many and finitely many <partial types> is exactly why Ryll-Nardzewski does not yield categoricity here.

= f
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Reuse the complete <free-bijection theory> from part (e). <Quantifier elimination> shows that every one-variable atomic statement is already decided by the no-finite-cycle axioms. Thus there is exactly one <complete one-type>, isolated by $x=x$.

For two <logical variables>, each <integer> $k$ gives a different <complete type> isolated by
$$
y=f^k(x).
$$
There is also the <complete type>
$$
p_\infty(x,y)=\{y\ne f^k(x):k\in\mathbb Z\},
$$
saying that the two elements lie in different orbits. These exhaust the possibilities by <quantifier elimination>. In particular
$$
\boxed{|S_1(T_{\mathbb Z})|=1,\qquad |S_2(T_{\mathbb Z})|=\aleph_0.}
$$
The last <complete type> is a <nonprincipal complete type>. Any <logical formula> uses only finitely many <integer> offsets after <quantifier elimination>; a pair in a single orbit with a sufficiently different offset agrees with a different-orbit pair on those tests. Hence no single <logical formula> can isolate $p_\infty$.

The one-orbit <first-order model> $(\mathbb Z,f(n)=n+1,g(n)=n-1)$ omits $p_\infty$, while a two-orbit <first-order model> realizes it. This illustrates the <omitting types theorem> and shows concretely why finitely many <complete one-types> alone cannot replace the all-arities condition in Ryll-Nardzewski.
